&lt;h3&gt;What you’ll work on&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bring your curiosity, bright ideas and problem-solving skills to the chips behind tomorrow’s AI systems.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a Graduate Silicon Engineer, you’ll join one of our four specialist Silicon teams: logical design, verification, physical design or design for test (DFT). You’ll contribute to real chip development, learning from experienced colleagues and building your understanding of how advanced AI chips are designed, verified and prepared for manufacture.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Depending on your team, you’ll work on areas such as register-transfer level (RTL) design, SOC integration, verification methods, physical implementation or the critical role of DFT. You’ll use industry-standard electronic design automation (EDA) tools and take ownership of clear tasks, with support from experienced engineers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;How you’ll learn and grow&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During the two-year programme, you’ll develop practical silicon engineering skills in the team you join, building on the fundamentals you’ve learned through your studies and projects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hands-on tasks will help you learn modern design techniques and use industry-standard EDA tools. You’ll build skills in areas such as RTL, verification, timing or physical design, depending on your team’s focus.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Feedback from reviews and practical problem solving will help you develop your technical judgement, explain your approach and understand how your work fits into the wider chip development process. Bring your curiosity and willingness to learn; we’ll support you as you build your knowledge and confidence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What we&#39;re looking for&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On track for, or achieved, a degree in Electronic Engineering, Computer Science or a related subject such as Physics, Maths&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Good coding skills such as C, C++ or Python&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An interest in digital design, application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs), verification, physical design, computer architecture or AI compute, and motivation to learn modern silicon design techniques and EDA tools&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
